# Lab / Runner Handoff Contract

- Runtime truth and research authority have separate scopes. `quant-runner` is the sole source of instance authorization and actual on/off state through its code, committed config/generated lock, service state, and runtime ledger/telemetry. Lab Markdown records family/version identity, research state, evidence, and handoff contracts only; Lab does not keep an authorization manifest.
- Changing runner enabled state, mode, live/dry-run config, generated lock, or any real service state is an operational decision reserved to the user. An AI must not infer enable, disable, restart, stop, or demotion from a missing/deleted document, artifact, failed CI check, stale inventory, or status inconsistency. Without an explicit user decision, preserve the runner configuration, record the research discrepancy, and ask before any operational mutation.
- Standard parity artifacts are normative research evidence and should exist in a clean checkout when cited. Missing or untracked evidence blocks new promotion claims but does not itself change runner authorization or runtime. Do not reconstruct evidence from prose or fabricate a replacement.
- The generated lock is owned and interpreted only by the sibling `quant-runner` repository at `/Users/ZK/OpenCode/quant-runner/configs/active-strategy.lock.json`; Lab neither generates nor authorizes it.
- The sibling repository `/Users/ZK/OpenCode/quant-runner` is the only production execution surface. Research backtest scripts in this repository must never be promoted into runners.
- A strategy may only be handed to `quant-runner` through a runner handoff spec: a Markdown file under the family's `live-specs/` directory whose status line explicitly states the promotion state (for example `dry-run / not live-ready` or `live spec / not live-ready`). `live spec` is optional; `registered -> dry-run` is allowed. Live-executable audit and hard gates are required before `live`; `dry-run` only requires runner reproducibility and `runner-tracking/`.
- `handoff` is an overlay label only. It may annotate `live spec`, `dry-run`, or `live`, but never acts as a main status, skips a gate, or authorizes an instance.
- Legacy external-runner observations belong in family ledgers and runner-tracking reports as historical evidence only; Lab keeps no external authorization registry. A recorded external instance must not be copied, changed, restarted, stopped, or migrated without an explicit user decision; all supported current authorization lives in `quant-runner`.
- Every runner handoff spec must contain at minimum: full family name and version, runner strategy `kind` (matching the actual `quant-runner` config value), exchange/market/symbol/timeframe, a complete parameter table with the exact runner config field names, data-quality requirements and warmup needs, fee/slippage/funding assumptions, closed-bar-only requirement, a runner TOML example, the remaining audit gaps, and links to the core ledger and the supporting research evidence.
- Live notional is not a required field in strategy specs. If live sizing is controlled by a funded subaccount rather than by strategy parameters, record the funding/risk boundary in runner operations or the launch decision log instead of forcing it into the spec.
- Runner-side identity must stay in sync: the handoff spec's `kind`, module path, and TOML example must match the current `quant-runner` code and configs. When `quant-runner` renames a kind or moves a module, update the lab-side handoff spec in the same change set.
- The `quant-runner` strategy directory keeps its own `<STRATEGY_ID>-SPEC.md`; the lab handoff spec and the runner SPEC must link to each other. Parameter changes must be applied to both or explicitly recorded as divergence in the family decision log.
- Lab live SPEC front matter must validate against `docs/research-governance/schemas/lab-live-spec-frontmatter.schema.json`; new specs should start from `docs/research-governance/lab-live-spec-template.md`. A single implementation uses `strategy_id`, `runner_kind`, and `peer_spec`. A genuine joint SPEC uses an `implementations` array containing one explicit `strategy_id`/`runner_kind`/`peer_spec` mapping per route; parallel scalar lists or implicit peer kinds are forbidden. All active SPECs also declare `schema_version`, `spec_role`, `family_id`, `main_status`, `spec_status`, and `approval_level_max`.
- A `(strategy_id, runner_kind)` mapping may have only one active Lab SPEC and one active runner implementation SPEC. Historical specifications remain readable but must be marked `superseded`.
- Runner observations must flow back: the `runner-tracking/` subdirectory must be created in the same change that marks a version `dry-run`, and it holds dated Markdown reports (`<family-id>-runner-<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`). The standard reflux entry is quant-runner `scripts/lab_sync/sync_to_lab.sh`; DRAFT reports are not gate evidence until human review. Each report states the runner config used, the observation window, trade count, realized fees/slippage versus backtest assumptions, signal/indicator parity mismatches, incidents (restarts, missing bars, order rejections), and a keep/stop/adjust conclusion. This repository does not use an additional simulated-live state; simulation is `dry-run`, and real-order small-notional execution is `live`.
- Online open/close reconciliation is a mandatory runner-tracking trigger: when live or dry-run open/close/fill statistics are extracted to compare runtime behavior with research backtests, write a dated `runner-tracking/` report and retain any durable CSV/JSON evidence under the same family's `artifacts/`. The report must include the source command/log/DB snapshot, observation window, runner config, signal/bar timestamps, expected backtest entry/exit, actual open/close timestamps, side, quantity/notional, fill/mark/reference prices, fees, slippage estimate, order IDs or stable event IDs when available, and a match/mismatch conclusion.
- `dry-run -> live` admission requires a completed online open/close reconciliation report with no unresolved material mismatch. A smoke test, offline replay parity, or narrative statement alone cannot satisfy this gate.
- Statements like `forward-test required` in a core ledger are only satisfied by reports under `runner-tracking/`; verbal claims about dry-run performance do not count as evidence.
- The family core ledger must reference the latest runner-tracking report whenever the version's status depends on runner evidence, and any promotion or demotion driven by runner observations must be recorded in the family `decision-log.md`.
